positive_text:
\text{Dado un cuadrilátero convexo } ABCD \text{ y una copia congruente } A'B'C'D', \text{ se corta } ABCD \text{ a lo largo de la diagonal } AC \text{ y } A'B'C'D' \text{ a lo largo de la diagonal } B'D'. \text{ Se obtienen cuatro triángulos.}

\text{a) Da un método general, independiente de la forma de } ABCD, \text{ para formar un paralelogramo con los cuatro triángulos.}

\text{b) Si los lados del cuadrilátero son } 3, 3, 4 \text{ y } 6, \text{ muestra que el perímetro del paralelogramo construido está entre } 16 \text{ y } 28, \text{ sin importar el orden de los lados.}

Let $M = \{1, 2, \dots, 2013\}$ and let $\Gamma$ be a circle. Assign to each subset $\mathcal{A} \subseteq M$ a distinct point $A$ on $\Gamma$ such that:
1. Each distinct subset corresponds to a distinct point;
2. All assigned points form the vertices of a regular polygon;
3. For any $k > 2$, if $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_k$ form a regular $k$-gon, then $S(\mathcal{A}_1) + S(\mathcal{A}_2) + \dots + S(\mathcal{A}_k)$ is divisible by 2014, where $S(\mathcal{A})$ is the sum of elements in $\mathcal{A}$ and $S(\emptyset) = 0$.

A positive integer $ n $ is called *super special* if there exist positive integers $ x, y, u, v $ such that $ n = \frac{2y^3 + x^3}{2v^3 + u^3} $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

negatives.text:
A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 - 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $, with the additional constraint that $ x = u $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2015 is not super special.

positive_text:
In a forest with $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$), each living in a distinct cave connected by a unique path between every pair. During the election campaign, each animal that campaigns performs a complete tour visiting all other caves exactly once, using only the given paths and never switching paths between caves, returning to its own cave at the end. Moreover, each path is used by at most one campaign animal.

a) Prove that if $n$ is a prime number, then the maximum number of such campaign animals is $\frac{n-1}{2}$;

b) Determine the maximum number of campaign animals when $n=9$.

negatives.text:
Imagine a forest with $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$), each living in a different cave. There is exactly one unique path connecting any two caves. Before the King of the Forest election, some animals go on a campaign. Each campaigning animal visits all the other caves exactly once, only using the paths, never switching paths between caves, and returns to its own cave at the end. Also, no path is used by more than one campaigning animal.

a) Show that if $n$ is a prime number, then the most animals that can go on a campaign is $\frac{n}{2}$;

b) How many animals can go on a campaign when $n=9$?

Imagine a forest with $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$), each living in a different cave. There is exactly one unique path connecting any two caves. Before the King of the Forest election, some animals go on a campaign. Each campaigning animal visits all the other caves exactly once, only using the paths, never switching paths between caves, and returns to its own cave at the end. Also, no path is used by more than one campaigning animal.

a) Show that if $n$ is a prime number, then the most animals that can go on a campaign is $\frac{n-1}{3}$;

b) How many animals can go on a campaign when $n=9$?

Imagine a forest with $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$), each living in a different cave. There is exactly one unique path connecting any two caves. Before the King of the Forest election, some animals go on a campaign. Each campaigning animal visits all the other caves exactly once, only using the paths, never switching paths between caves, and returns to its own cave at the end. Also, no path is used by more than one campaigning animal.

a) Show that if $n$ is a prime number, then the most animals that can go on a campaign is $\frac{n-2}{2}$;

b) How many animals can go on a campaign when $n=9$?

\text{Let } \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ be the set of positive rational numbers. Find all functions } f: \mathbb{Q}_{+} \rightarrow \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ which for all } x \in \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ satisfy}

(1) \quad f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) = f(x)

(2) \quad \left(1 - \frac{1}{x}\right) f(x) = f(x + 1)

\text{Let } \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ be the set of positive rational numbers. Find all functions } f: \mathbb{Q}_{+} \rightarrow \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ which for all } x \in \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ satisfy}

(1) \quad f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) = f(x)

(2) \quad \left(1 + \frac{1}{x}\right) f(x) = f(x - 1)

\text{Let } \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ be the set of positive rational numbers. Find all functions } f: \mathbb{Q}_{+} \rightarrow \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ which for all } x \in \mathbb{Q}_{+} \text{ satisfy}

(1) \quad f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) = 2f(x)

(2) \quad \left(1 + \frac{1}{x}\right) f(x) = f(x + 1)

positive_text:
A committee of 25 countries operates with the following conditions:

(1) meetings occur daily;

(2) every meeting has at least one country present;

(3) no two meetings have the same set of countries attending; and

(4) for any meeting $n$, each previous meeting $k < n$ must have shared at least one common country with the current meeting.

What is the maximum number of consecutive days the committee can hold meetings?

negatives.text:
The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should include at least one state that was not represented at the $k$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should include at least one state that was represented at the $n$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should include at least two states that were represented at the $k$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

positive_text:
Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be real non-zero numbers ($b > 0$) such that the quadratic $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ shares two distinct roots with the cubic $x^3 + bx^2 + ax + c = 0$. Show that

a) $abc > 16$;

b) $abc \ge \frac{3125}{108}$.

On a chessboard of size $n \times n$, each cell contains a beetle. At 12:00, each beetle moves to a cell that shares a side with its current location. Determine the maximum number of cells that can be left completely empty after the move for
a) $n = 8$;
b) $n = 9$.

negatives.text:
Imagine a chessboard with $n \times n$ squares. Each square has one bug sitting on it. At noon, every bug moves to a neighboring square (two squares are neighbors if they share a side). Determine the maximum number of bugs that can remain on the board if

a) $n = 8$;

b) $n = 9$.

Imagine a chessboard with $n \times n$ squares. Each square has one bug sitting on it. At noon, every bug moves to a neighboring square (two squares are neighbors if they share a side). Determine the maximum number of squares that can be occupied by more than one bug after the move if

a) $n = 8$;

b) $n = 9$.

Imagine a chessboard with $n \times n$ squares. Each square has one bug sitting on it. At noon, every bug moves to a neighboring square (two squares are neighbors if they share a side). Determine the maximum number of squares that can remain unchanged (i.e., with the same number of bugs) if

a) $n = 8$;

b) $n = 9$.

Find all real numbers $a$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
1) $f(f(x)) = x f(x) + a x$ for all real $x$;
2) $f$ is not constant;
3) $f$ takes the value $a$.

Find all real numbers $a$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
1) $f(f(x)) = x f(x) - a x$ for all real $x$;
2) $f$ is constant;
3) $f$ takes the value $a$.

Find all real numbers $a$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:
1) $f(f(x)) = x f(x) - a x$ for all real $x$;
2) $f$ is not constant;
3) $f$ takes the value $0$.

Considere um anel formado por $m$ polígonos regulares, cada um com $n$ lados. As regras para formar esse anel são:
i) cada polígono toca dois outros no anel;
ii) dois polígonos que estão lado a lado compartilham apenas um lado;
iii) a borda da área no centro do anel é formada por exatamente um lado de cada polígono.
Na imagem, temos um exemplo com $m=6$ e $n=9$. Quantos valores diferentes de $n$ são possíveis para formar esse anel?

Considere um anel formado por $m$ polígonos regulares, cada um com $n$ lados. As regras para formar esse anel são:
i) cada polígono toca dois outros no anel;
ii) dois polígonos que estão lado a lado compartilham apenas dois lados;
iii) a borda da área no centro do anel é formada por exatamente dois lados de cada polígono.
Na imagem, temos um exemplo com $m=6$ e $n=9$. Quantos valores diferentes de $n$ são possíveis para formar esse anel?

Considere um anel formado por $m$ polígonos regulares, cada um com $n$ lados. As regras para formar esse anel são:
i) cada polígono toca dois outros no anel;
ii) dois polígonos que estão lado a lado compartilham apenas um lado;
iii) a borda da área no centro do anel é formada por exatamente dois lados de cada polígono.
Na imagem, temos um exemplo com $m=6$ e $n=9$. Quantos valores diferentes de $m$ são possíveis para formar esse anel?

positive_text:
