Find all integers $n \ge 3$ such that one can write a number (not necessarily an integer) into each vertex of a regular $n$-gon in such a way that both following conditions are met:
(1) Whenever three consecutive vertices of the $n$-gon, taken clockwise, contain numbers $x, y$ and $z$, respectively, the equality $x = y + z$ holds;
(2) The sum of the numbers in all vertices of the $n$-gon is 1.

Find all integers $n \ge 3$ such that one can write a number (not necessarily an integer) into each vertex of a regular $n$-gon in such a way that both following conditions are met:
(1) Whenever three consecutive vertices of the $n$-gon, taken clockwise, contain numbers $x, y$ and $z$, respectively, the equality $x = |y - z|$ holds;
(2) The product of the numbers in all vertices of the $n$-gon is 1.

Find all integers $n \ge 3$ such that one can write a number (not necessarily an integer) into each vertex of a regular $n$-gon in such a way that both following conditions are met:
(1) Whenever three consecutive vertices of the $n$-gon, taken clockwise, contain numbers $x, y$ and $z$, respectively, the equality $x = |y - z|$ holds;
(2) The sum of the numbers in all vertices of the $n$-gon is 0.

positive_text:
Find all lists $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $a_{1} \leq a_{2} \leq \ldots \leq a_{2020}$;
(ii) $a_{2020} - a_{1} \leq 1$;
(iii) there is a permutation $\left(b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(a_{i}+1\right)\left(b_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2}=8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} a_{i}^{3}
$$

negatives.text:
Find all sequences $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following conditions are satisfied:
(1) $a_{1} \leq a_{2} \leq \ldots \leq a_{2020}$;
(2) $a_{2020} \leq a_{1}+1$;
(3) there is a rearrangement $\left(b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(a_{i}+1\right)\left(b_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2} = 4 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} a_{i}^{3}
$$
A rearrangement means the same numbers in a different order. For instance, $\left(2,1,2\right)$ is a rearrangement of $\left(1,2,2\right)$, and they are both rearrangements of $\left(2,2,1\right)$. Note that any sequence is a rearrangement of itself.

Find all sequences $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following conditions are satisfied:
(1) $a_{1} \leq a_{2} \leq \ldots \leq a_{2020}$;
(2) $a_{2020} \leq a_{1}+1$;
(3) there is a rearrangement $\left(b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(a_{i}+1\right)\left(b_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2} = 8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} a_{i}^{2}
$$
A rearrangement means the same numbers in a different order. For instance, $\left(2,1,2\right)$ is a rearrangement of $\left(1,2,2\right)$, and they are both rearrangements of $\left(2,2,1\right)$. Note that any sequence is a rearrangement of itself.

Find all sequences $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that the following conditions are satisfied:
(1) $a_{1} \leq a_{2} \leq \ldots \leq a_{2020}$;
(2) $a_{2020} \leq a_{1}+1$;
(3) there is a rearrangement $\left(b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ such that
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(a_{i}+2\right)\left(b_{i}+2\right)\right)^{2} = 8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} a_{i}^{3}
$$
A rearrangement means the same numbers in a different order. For instance, $\left(2,1,2\right)$ is a rearrangement of $\left(1,2,2\right)$, and they are both rearrangements of $\left(2,2,1\right)$. Note that any sequence is a rearrangement of itself.

positive_text:
Soit $k \geq 3$ un entier. On définit la suite $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ par $a_{k}=2k$, et
$$
a_{n} = 
\begin{cases}
a_{n-1}+1 & \text{ si } \operatorname{pgcd}(n, a_{n-1}) = 1 \\
2n & \text{ sinon. }
\end{cases}
$$

Démontrer que la suite $\left(a_{n+1} - a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ contient une infinité de nombres premiers.

negatives.text:
Soit $k \geq 3$ un entier. On définit la suite $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ par $a_{k} = 2k$, et
$$
a_{n} = 
\begin{cases}
\ a_{n-1} + 1 & \text{ si } \operatorname{pgcd}(a_{n-1}, n) = 1 \\
\ n & \text{ sinon. }
\end{cases}
$$
Montrer que la suite $\left(a_{n+1} - a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ a une infinité de termes qui sont des nombres premiers.

Soit $k \geq 3$ un entier. On définit la suite $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ par $a_{k} = 2k$, et
$$
a_{n} = 
\begin{cases}
\ a_{n-1} + 1 & \text{ si } \operatorname{pgcd}(a_{n-1}, n) = 1 \\
\ 2n & \text{ sinon. }
\end{cases}
$$
Montrer que la suite $\left(a_{n+1} + a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ a une infinité de termes qui sont des nombres premiers.

Soit $k \geq 3$ un entier. On définit la suite $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ par $a_{k} = 2k$, et
$$
a_{n} = 
\begin{cases}
\ a_{n-1} + 1 & \text{ si } \operatorname{pgcd}(a_{n-1}, n) = 1 \\
\ 2n & \text{ sinon. }
\end{cases}
$$
Montrer que la suite $\left(a_{n+1} - a_{n}\right)_{n \geq k}$ a une infinité de termes pairs.

positive_text:
Soit $ABC$ un triangle. Soient $E$ sur $[AB]$ et $D$ sur $[AC]$ tels que $BE = CD$. On note $P$ le point d'intersection de $(BD)$ et $(CE)$. Les cercles circonscrits à $BEP$ et $CDP$ se coupent en un second point $Q$. Si $K$ et $L$ sont les milieux respectifs de $[BE]$ et $[CD]$, et si $R$ est l'intersection des perpendiculaires à $(QK)$ et $(QL)$ passant respectivement par $K$ et $L$, démontrer que

1) $R$ appartient au cercle circonscrit de $ABC$;
2) $Q$ est situé sur la bissectrice de $\widehat{BAC}$.

negatives.text:
Soit $ABC$ un triangle. Soient $D \in [AC]$ et $E \in [AB]$ tels que $BE = CD$. Soit $P$ le point d'intersection de $(BD)$ et $(CE)$. Les cercles circonscrits à $BEP$ et $CDP$ se recoupent en $Q$. Soient $K$ et $L$ les milieux respectifs de $[BE]$ et $[CD]$. Soit $R$ l'intersection entre la perpendiculaire à $(QK)$ passant par $K$ et la perpendiculaire à $(QL)$ passant par $L$. Montrer que

1) $R$ se trouve sur la médiatrice de $[BC]$;
2) $Q$ se trouve sur la bissectrice de l'angle $\widehat{BAC}$.

