- per ogni $n$, $f(n)$ è la somma di $f(p)$ al variare di $p$ tra i primi che dividono $n$.
Trova il massimo e il minimo valore possibile per $f(2020)$, al variare di $f$ tra le funzioni primordiali.

positive_text:
\text{Sia } x_{0} \text{un numero reale positivo. Si definisce una successione tale che } x_{n+1} = \left\{1 - \frac{1}{x_{n}}\right\}, \text{dove } \{\cdot\} \text{denota la parte frazionaria. La successione termina quando } x_{n} = 0.

a. \text{Mostrare che se } x_{0} = \frac{a}{b} \text{con } a, b \in \mathbb{N}^{+}, \text{allora la successione finisce in un numero finito di passi.}

b. \text{Dimostrare che se la successione termina, allora } x_{0} \text{è razionale.}

\text{There are } n > 1 \text{ pirates of different ages who together possess 2009 coins. Each pirate has one more coin than the next younger pirate, except the youngest.}

\text{a) Determine all values of } n \text{ that satisfy this condition.}

\text{b) On each day, a pirate is selected to give one coin to each of the other pirates. If } n = 7, \text{ what is the maximum number of coins a pirate can have after some number of days?}

There are $n$ individuals, each initially holding a unique item. Through a series of pairwise exchanges, where each exchange involves two individuals swapping their current items, every pair of individuals must have exchanged at least once. After all exchanges, each person ends up with their original item. Determine the minimal number of exchanges required, for:
a) $n=5$;
b) $n=6$.

negatives.text:
A group of $n$ children each has a unique toy. They take turns trading toys: in each trade, two children exchange the toys they are holding at that moment. Every pair of children must trade at least once. After all the trades, each child ends up with a different toy than they started with. What is the minimum number of trades required, if:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$?

A group of $n$ children each has a unique toy. They take turns trading toys: in each trade, two children exchange the toys they are holding at that moment. Every pair of children must trade at least once. After all the trades, each child ends up with the toy they started with, but one pair of children must trade an extra time for some reason. What is the minimum number of trades required, if:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$?

A group of $n$ children each has a unique toy. They take turns trading toys: in each trade, two children exchange the toys they are holding at that moment. Each pair of children must trade at least once, and the number of trades must be even. After all the trades, each child ends up with the same toy they started with. What is the minimum number of trades required, if:

a) $n=5$;

b) $n=6$?

