Let $a_{11}, a_{21}, \ldots, a_{n1}$, $n>2$, be distinct integers. Assume that the values $a_{1k}, a_{2k}, \ldots, a_{nk}$ have been defined for some $k$. Define
$$
\begin{aligned}
& a_{i, k+1} = \frac{a_{ik} + a_{i+1, k}}{2}, \quad i = 1, 2, \ldots, n-1 \\
& a_{n, k+1} = \frac{a_{nk} + a_{1k}}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
Prove that for odd $n$, there exist indices $j, k$ such that $a_{jk}$ is not an integer. What happens if $n$ is even?

